WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.900
In 2004, the average person could focus

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:06.520
for 2 and 1/2 minutes. Today, it's 47

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:08.840
[music]

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:11.120
seconds. You have to fix your attention

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:13.600
span before it's too late. Because if

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:16.800
you can't focus, you can't learn. And in

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:20.080
the age of AI, that makes you obsolete.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:22.840
As a CEO, board member, advisor to

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:24.640
billion-dollar companies, I had to learn

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:27.120
to [music] protect my attention like a

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:29.320
weapon. So, today I'm giving you a

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:32.119
science-backed system to build that deep

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:34.160
focus and become immune to distractions.

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:35.680
Let's get into it. First, you really

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:38.600
have to understand who you're up

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:41.840
against. In 2011, a New York Times

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:44.360
reporter asked Steve Jobs if his kids

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:46.320
loved the iPad. And his answer, "They

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:48.720
haven't used it. We limit how much

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:50.520
technology our kids use at home." And

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:53.320
think about it, the man who built the

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:56.240
iPad didn't let his own kids touch it.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:59.840
And he's not alone. Gates, Zuckerberg,

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:02.640
the Snapchat CEO, all of them restrict

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:04.680
screen time for their kids. Sean Parker,

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:07.280
Facebook's first president, said the

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:10.040
goal was to consume as much of your time

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:12.760
and attention by giving you that little

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:15.600
dopamine head every once in a while. And

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:18.440
it's working. Last year alone, Google

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.565
and Meta earned nearly half a trillion

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:22.760
dollars by selling your attention.

00:01:20.565 --> 00:01:25.160
[music] But here's the part that keeps

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:28.760
me up at night. It's not even humans

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:30.880
anymore. Today, AI systems decide what

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:33.880
to serve next so they can keep your

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:36.160
attention hijacked. You and [music] I

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:38.280
are just inventory to be auctioned on

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:41.160
the internet every second. This

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:43.040
trillion-dollar heist is their business

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:45.000
model. If you ask these companies, they

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:46.920
would say, "Well, the ads keep the

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.041
internet free." There's some truth to

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:50.120
it. Content is free, but your attention

00:01:50.041 --> 00:01:52.720
>> [music]

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:55.440
>> is the prisoner because you're fighting

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:58.960
the most sophisticated psychological

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:01.800
machinery ever built and optimized by

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.360
algorithms that know you better than you

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:05.920
know yourself. You're not just

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:08.160
distracted, you're being harvested. And

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:10.440
the scariest part, it has already

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:13.000
changed the way our brain focuses. And

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:16.400
we can measure it. Gloria Mark has

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:18.206
tracked attention for 20 years. In 2004,

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:20.560
it was measured in minutes. Today,

00:02:18.206 --> 00:02:22.680
[music] 47 seconds. That's it. And this

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:25.880
is even more interesting. She found that

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:28.880
we self-interrupt 49% of the time. It's

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:31.000
not just notifications, half the time

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:33.920
you're doing it to yourself. I see it in

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.880
my life. When ChatGPT or Gemini is

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:38.680
thinking for a few seconds, I

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:41.360
immediately reach out for my phone or

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:43.520
switch to another tab. I tell myself I'm

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:46.480
being productive, but I'm trained to see

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:49.280
that 10-second gap [music] as a cue to

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:52.640
switch. And you see this everywhere. In

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:55.560
elevators, in lines, the moment the

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:58.240
world goes quiet, all of us reach for

00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:00.960
that screen. We've become terrified of

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:04.120
that gap. This is the scariest metric.

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:05.720
The average American checks their phone

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:07.880
186

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:10.920
times a day. Now, factor in the

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:14.040
switching tax of 23 minutes that it

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:16.680
takes to regain deep focus after an

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:19.760
interruption, and your day isn't just

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:22.960
busy, it's shredded into fragmentation.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:26.320
So, the question isn't willpower. To win

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:29.280
that 47-second war, you redesign the

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:31.840
system. You guard the gate. Here are the

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:33.960
three levers that can reset your focus.

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:36.400
The first mistake is thinking that focus

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:39.000
is a willpower problem. In a research

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:41.440
study often called brain drain, three

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:44.360
groups of people were given the same

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:47.120
thinking tasks. The only difference was

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:49.800
where they put their phones. Group one,

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:52.840
on the desk. Group two, in their

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:54.560
pockets. Group three, in another room.

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:56.720
Now, all phones were off, but the

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:59.520
result, the group with the phone in that

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:02.760
other room completely dominated. If your

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:04.972
phone is in front of you, your brain is

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:07.480
burning cycles resisting it. It's

00:04:04.972 --> 00:04:10.920
[music] waiting. So, stop that fight

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:12.880
entirely. Remove the enemy. So, how do

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:15.000
you make this actionable? Here are the

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:17.880
three steps I call the three R

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:18.720
framework. Reach, roadblocks, and

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:21.760
rituals. [music]

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:24.120
First, reach. Keep the distraction out

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:26.880
of reach. That's it. Maya Angelou didn't

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:30.480
write at home. She rented a tiny,

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:33.840
anonymous hotel room, plain, quiet. She

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:36.560
made distractions physically impossible.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.760
If you want to build a masterpiece,

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:40.920
you can't do it in a room full of noise.

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:43.000
Keep your phone in another [music] room.

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:45.440
If it's in the room, a part of your

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:47.640
brain will always stay on standby for a

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:49.720
signal. Second, roadblock the

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:51.800
distraction. Make it expensive for

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:54.120
distractions to find you. So, look at

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:56.840
your phone, delete that useless app,

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:59.640
kill those notifications. Every layer of

00:04:56.840 --> 00:05:01.303
friction is a victory for your focus.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:01.440
Third, rituals.

00:05:01.303 --> 00:05:03.280
>> [music]

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:06.080
>> Now, they help you get straight into

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:08.000
action and not distraction. The NYU

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:11.960
psychologist Peter Gollwitzer found

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:14.880
this. Vague goals fail 62% of the time,

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:18.280
but rituals fail only 9% of the time.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:21.760
So, don't rely on trying harder, rely on

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:23.440
a trigger. Systems outlive self-control.

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:26.360
So, let me share with you my

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:28.120
trigger-based ritual. At night, I decide

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:30.760
what I will be working on tomorrow

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:33.440
morning. So, I leave that document or

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:36.080
that screen open on my laptop, and I

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:38.000
close everything else. Next morning,

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:40.200
when I turn it on, that's the first

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:42.560
thing I see. Nothing else is open.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:45.160
That's my focus loop. And you can create

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:47.520
your rituals to enter your focus loop.

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:50.320
But getting into that loop is a start.

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:52.680
Staying there is the real battle. For

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:55.680
that, you need the next framework. So,

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:58.000
here's how you reprogram your brain and

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:00.600
stay in deep focus. Every time you

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:03.000
scroll, every time you switch that

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:05.800
dopamine hit of novelty, you're carving

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:07.840
a groove in your brain. Do it for years,

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:10.040
and that groove becomes a canyon of

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:11.400
distraction. But neuroplasticity,

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:14.880
fortunately, is a two-way [music]

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:17.960
street. You train for 47-second bursts.

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:19.200
Now, you can train for 47-minute blocks.

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:22.760
One way to do [music] it is the

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:25.720
framework I call your SOS for attention.

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:29.200
Three simple steps. Sprint, offload,

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:31.280
save. First, sprint. Don't aim for 90

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:33.840
minutes of focus. Start [music] with 20,

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:36.280
even 15 minutes. When I first tried

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:38.920
this, I could barely hold focus for

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:41.680
maybe 7 minutes. That's how fragmented I

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:44.320
was. But now it's much longer. The

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:46.600
mental muscle grows over time. Think of

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:49.320
this as going to the gym. The starting

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:52.560
weight doesn't matter. Consistency does.

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:56.040
Second, offload. After a sprint, go

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:58.800
dark. No input, no phone. I focus on

00:06:56.040 --> 00:07:01.800
recovery. I sometimes just take a walk

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:04.680
or sit and [music] daydream. Gloria Mark

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:07.064
found that a 20-minute walk can recharge

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:07.160
your depleted cognitive battery

00:07:07.064 --> 00:07:10.200
>> [music]

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:13.760
>> immediately. So, let your mind offload

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:16.680
that weight. And finally, save. This is

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:19.040
the 10-second rule. Just 10 seconds of

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:21.800
stillness in the middle of a sprint

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:23.800
helps you encode data 20 times faster.

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:26.560
This was a surprise to me, too. But it's

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:28.800
the closest thing to a save button in

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:31.400
biology. It's where the learning

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:34.440
actually locks in. So, that's the SOS

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:36.760
framework. Because attention is not a

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:37.920
trade, it's a training. And in training,

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:40.200
you're not going to try to run a

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:41.780
marathon on day one. You're building the

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:41.800
ultimate superpower

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:44.320
>> [music]

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:47.600
>> for 2026. Because in a world of

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:50.400
47-second minds, the person who can hold

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:52.520
their focus owns the future. Now, let me

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:55.200
share why attention is the last

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:58.440
superpower. Even Superman has its

00:07:55.200 --> 00:08:00.280
fortress of solitude. Turns out, science

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:02.520
has a name for that state. A

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:05.160
psychologist named Mihaly

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:06.920
Csikszentmihalyi, and I hope I haven't

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:08.480
butchered that name. He's

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:10.840
Hungarian-American,

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:14.120
and he spent decades studying what he

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:18.000
called optimal experience. It's a state

00:08:14.120 --> 00:08:20.400
where time disappears and work feels

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:23.440
completely effortless, even when it's

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:26.320
hard. In the movie F1, this is exactly

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:29.160
what happens to Brad Pitt's character in

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:33.039
the end. The audience, the race, the

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:36.320
car, the driver, winning, losing,

00:08:33.039 --> 00:08:38.719
everything disappears. What remains is

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:40.760
just the road ahead.

00:08:38.719 --> 00:08:43.159
And yes, I have experienced that feeling

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:45.720
in my life several times. And it happens

00:08:43.159 --> 00:08:48.520
to all of us, right? The engineers,

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:51.680
musicians, surgeons, researchers,

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:55.640
painters, whoever you are. When we are

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:59.240
so deeply immersed, the self disappears.

00:08:55.640 --> 00:09:03.000
And surprisingly, that state of flow is

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:05.360
the key to everything we want. People in

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:06.840
flow states are more creative, more

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:09.600
productive, more [music]

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:12.480
deeply happy, and more sustainably

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:15.360
fulfilled because flow is where your

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:18.360
best work lives. But here's the catch.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:21.000
It often takes 15 to 20 minutes just to

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:24.400
even begin entering that flow state.

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:27.440
Flow is incompatible with a mind working

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:30.960
in 47-second bursts. Flow state happens

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:34.760
when you get past the surface and reach

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:38.000
that deeper space of inner harmony where

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:40.840
who you are and what you do become

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:43.560
inseparable. And this is so critical,

00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:46.520
even in our professions. Today, AI is

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:49.440
making all shallow work a commodity. In

00:09:46.520 --> 00:09:51.960
your career, in your business, your only

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:54.600
real edge is deep mastery. [music]

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:57.440
Anyone can skim the shallows, but can

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:59.640
you swim the deep oceans? Because if you

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:02.480
can't, AI will happily make you

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:05.040
replaceable. So, flow is so critical.

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:08.040
And I think it is critical emotionally,

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:11.120
too. In your relationships, every time

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:13.160
you half listen to someone while

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:15.840
glancing at your phone or a watch,

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:18.440
you're quietly eroding trust. You're

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:21.200
trading presence for performing.

00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:24.040
Attention is love made visible.

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:26.120
Distraction is how we withhold it. And

00:10:24.040 --> 00:10:28.600
attention is so critical for your peace

00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:31.920
of mind. The most meaningful questions

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:34.800
in life don't have quick 47-second

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:38.360
answers. They require sitting with hard

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:40.200
things for a long time. Long enough for

00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:42.280
a clarity to emerge. If you can't

00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:45.560
sustain attention, you can't sustain

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:47.840
inquiry, and you'll never know why you

00:10:45.560 --> 00:10:50.360
feel empty. That's the power of

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:53.960
attention. And here's why I think

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:56.880
attention is your greatest superpower.

00:10:53.960 --> 00:10:59.960
Years ago, I remember it like it was

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:03.160
yesterday. I was sitting on a bench

00:10:59.960 --> 00:11:06.120
watching kids play cricket in the park

00:11:03.160 --> 00:11:08.000
while my final exam on mathematics was

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:11.920
happening in the building right behind

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:15.000
me. I had walked out after the first 15

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:17.480
minutes. I read all the questions and

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:19.720
none of them made any [music] sense

00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:23.720
because I hadn't paid attention for

00:11:19.720 --> 00:11:26.320
years. And there I was, confused,

00:11:23.720 --> 00:11:27.040
trapped in the fog of my own mind, not

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:29.440
knowing [music]

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:31.880
where I'm going to go from there. But

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:34.960
someone eventually believed [music] in

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:37.400
me more than I believed in myself. They

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:39.040
loved me more than I loved myself. And

00:11:37.400 --> 00:11:42.560
most importantly, they paid [music]

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:45.480
attention to a lost soul. I felt seen.

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:48.440
That simple, generous act of [music]

00:11:45.480 --> 00:11:50.040
attention changed that kid's life, my

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:52.200
life.

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:54.880
That was the turning point. If

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:56.960
distraction is today's kryptonite,

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:59.520
attention is the last greatest

00:11:56.960 --> 00:12:02.080
superpower. If you want to love what you

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:04.760
do [music] and want to do what you love,

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:06.640
you can't afford to be distracted. It's

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:09.023
what builds your career, your

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:10.520
relationships, your sense of self. It

00:12:09.023 --> 00:12:14.160
[music] changes you.

00:12:10.520 --> 00:12:15.573
And as your final act of generosity,

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:16.880
when you pay attention to someone

00:12:15.573 --> 00:12:20.160
[music] else,

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:22.960
you will give them the same gift that

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:26.120
saved me. If you like this video, please

00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:27.400
subscribe and check out this next one.

00:12:26.120 --> 00:12:30.080
Thank you.

00:12:27.400 --> 00:12:30.080
And I love you.

